\chapter[Supplementary Results and Correctness Checks]{Supplementary Results\\and Correctness Checks}
\label{app:supplementary}
This appendix records parameter coverage, stage-specific validation and
secondary outcomes supporting Chapters 3--8. Measurement families retain
their own versions and timing boundaries. Their source mapping is in
Appendix~\ref{app:reproducibility}.

\section{Complete Parameter Registry}
\label{sec:b-grids}
The E1 comparison contains 1,590 valid measured configurations, with
coverage in \cref{tab:b-e1-counts}. Each point's complete parameter JSON,
recall, host-to-host QPS and source filename is retained in
\path{data/appendix/e1-configurations.csv}. The registry is generated by
\path{scripts/build_appendix.py}, with source identity recorded in
\path{data/appendix/audit.json}. The sets below describe observed values;
their Cartesian products are not assumed to have been measured.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\captionof{table}[Exact E1 configuration counts]{Exact E1 configuration counts, including coverage
extensions. Counts are parameter points, not independent index builds.}
\label{tab:b-e1-counts}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\input{tables/appendix/e1-counts}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

\begingroup\raggedright
\paragraph{JHQ-GPU.}
All E1 points use $B=B_r=8$, $M=d/8$, $k=10$ and batch 1,000.
Vogue uses 4,096 IVF lists; the other three use 8,192.
Vogue observes probes 16/\allowbreak{}32/\allowbreak{}64/\allowbreak{}96/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}192/\allowbreak{}256/\allowbreak{}384/\allowbreak{}512 and
$\alpha\in\{16,32,64,128\}$ over 27 tuples. ArXiv adds probe 1,024
and uses $\alpha\in\{25,50,100,200\}$ over 29 tuples.
Both OpenAI grids use probes 32/\allowbreak{}64/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}256/\allowbreak{}512/\allowbreak{}1,024;
their observed multipliers are respectively
$\{4,8,16,32,64,128,256,512\}$ and
$\{2,4,8,16,32,64,128,256\}$, over 37 and 39 tuples.
The reported scheduled path uses four groups on Vogue and one block
elsewhere. OpenAI uses TF32 rotation/centroid GEMMs in historical v69;
the v72 FP32 sensitivity is a separate family (\cref{sec:7-precision}).

\paragraph{CAGRA fp32 and int8.}
Each representation has 64 points per dataset: graph degrees 32/\allowbreak{}64,
intermediate degree 128, \texttt{ivf\_pq} construction,
\texttt{itopk\_size} in 32/\allowbreak{}64/\allowbreak{}96/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}192/\allowbreak{}256/\allowbreak{}384/\allowbreak{}512 and search width
1/\allowbreak{}2/\allowbreak{}4/\allowbreak{}8. Int8 records quantile 0.99 for dataset scaling and includes
query quantisation in the host search boundary.

\paragraph{IVF-PQ.}
Eight-bit subcodes use 192/\allowbreak{}384/\allowbreak{}768-byte codes at dimension 768,
and 384/\allowbreak{}768/\allowbreak{}1,536-byte codes on OpenAI. Each length has fp16/fp32
lookup tables and probes 16/\allowbreak{}32/\allowbreak{}64/\allowbreak{}96/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}192/\allowbreak{}256/\allowbreak{}384/\allowbreak{}512:
54 points per dataset. The recorded refinement ratio is one and list
counts match JHQ. Code lengths and memory budgets differ.

\paragraph{IVF-RaBitQ and brute force.}
RaBitQ records 4/\allowbreak{}8/\allowbreak{}9 bits per dimension and probes
16/\allowbreak{}32/\allowbreak{}64/\allowbreak{}96/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}192/\allowbreak{}256/\allowbreak{}384/\allowbreak{}512/\allowbreak{}768/\allowbreak{}1,024. The 768-dimensional and
OpenAI-1536 records contain LUT16/LUT32/QUANT4/QUANT8 modes;
OpenAI-3072 contains LUT32/QUANT4/QUANT8. Additional 4,096-list OpenAI
indexes extend the default 8,192-list coverage. Exact combinations are
in the registry. Each dataset also has one FP32 squared-L2 brute-force
point. An absent mode or higher-recall point is not a measured failure.

\paragraph{Auxiliary grids.}
LUT evaluation fixes $\alpha=100$ and probes 8/\allowbreak{}32/\allowbreak{}128/\allowbreak{}512 on six
datasets with $\glut\in\{1,2,4,8\}$: 24 cells and 96 timed arms.
Budget evaluation uses probes 128/\allowbreak{}512 and
$A=(200,100,64,32,16,8,4,2)$ on the same six datasets, with 64 draws
at each $S\in\{32,64,128\}$ and $\epsilon=1$. FULL separately uses
all 500 calibration queries. Held-out counts are 500, or 499 for
Stella. Direct reuse tests four cells, four policies and five rounds
of 500 repeated held-out batches. Boundaries are specified in
\cref{sec:a-budget-contract,sec:a-supplement}.

\par\endgroup

\section{Correctness and Numerical Evidence}
\label{sec:b-correctness}
\Cref{tab:b-checks} separates numerical, algorithmic and memory evidence.
Chapter 5's ordering arguments concern computed scores under their
stated contracts; raw-vector recall is evaluated separately.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\captionof{table}[Validation coverage]{Validation coverage and the object actually checked.}
\label{tab:b-checks}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{4pt}
\begin{tabularx}{\linewidth}{@{}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}p{.22\linewidth}>{\raggedright\arraybackslash}X@{}}
\toprule
Boundary & Completed evidence and scope \\
\midrule
Representation & Four all-row offset/ID audits; sampled raw-input FP64
recoding and stored-matrix geometry; TF32 deviations identified \\
Cartesian LUTs & Real-arithmetic factorisation; 96 timed arms; T4
per-candidate FP32 error and ranking diagnostics across four table forms \\
Global selection & T4: 270 configurations, 69,120 query instances;
55,296 grouped instances match strict CPU complete-key sorting in order \\
Shared bound & 48 paired checks; extracted scalar 64-bit PTX and toolkit
SASS support whole-key access; actual driver SASS not captured \\
Scheduling & 250 variant/configuration and 234,320 query comparisons,
retaining distinctions between ID sets and tie order \\
Probe selector & 1,792 synthetic cases with zero failures; real-query
ID/offset checks, relative to computed routing scores \\
Budget search & 2,304 monotone sampled curves and 12 FULL enumeration
matches; 18,432 sampled grid counts reproduce full-pool subset sums \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

The smaller representation-audit databases have identical primary and
residual bytes across one-/two-pass builds when joined by ID.
Corrections differ by at most $8.94\times10^{-8}$ and 1/\allowbreak{}1/\allowbreak{}40 rows change
list assignment; Stella validates its natural two-pass path
(\cref{sec:4-representation-audit}). T4 now supplies the strict
computed-score selection reference (\cref{sec:a-t4-t5}). The controlled
same-model Vogue cross-process diagnosis remains unperformed: archived
processes with matching probe/primary dumps still report final recall
0.9642/\allowbreak{}0.9644. Within-run selection checks do not localise
that separate anomaly.

\section{Secondary Results and Evidence Status}
\label{sec:b-secondary}
\paragraph{Adverse regimes.}
The scheduler's largest recorded regret is $1.217\times$ at batch 256.
Scheduled/one-block throughput on Vogue spans 0.895--1.175 in E1:
the skew rule enabling its recall-0.97 lead also adds overhead in some
low-probe cells (\cref{sec:7-6}). These adverse results accompany the
favourable internal scheduling findings. In T5's cross-system comparison,
CAGRA fp32 leads on all four datasets at recall 0.97 and batch 8;
batch-32 near ties depend on the recall estimator
(\cref{sec:7-smallbatch}).

\paragraph{Representation alternatives.}
Mailman-style GPU scorers are isolated kernel measurements; their
end-to-end replacements are optimistic models. The rank-oriented study
is an FP64 offline replay. A separate median-code GPU follow-up improves
arXiv at the recorded target by $1.402\times$ at batch 8 and
$1.208\times$ at batch 1,000, misses Vogue's strict target and is nearly
tied on OpenAI-3072. Thresholds, levels and residual training change
together, preventing a threshold-only attribution (\cref{sec:8-2}).

\paragraph{Calibration and precision.}
Finite-pool prediction explains sampling risk conditional on stable
query counts; its GT-loss mapping uses held-out fixed-arm quality.
Direct reuse measures cumulative cost on four repeated pools.
The precision intervention changes add/query GEMMs on one TF32-trained
cache. Full FP32 retraining, future-query drift, cross-device rankings
and online updates are outside these experiments.

\section{Construction Capacities and Precision Anchors}
\label{sec:b-full-review}

\Cref{tab:b-scratch} separates input staging from independently allocated
assignment products. Source defaults are three buffers, 65,536 configured
add rows and 128\,MiB per input/rotated float array. These are allocation
capacities, not current sub-batch sizes or total process peaks.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\captionof{table}[Independent construction-buffer capacities]{Default construction-buffer capacities.
The last column is $4n_b n_{\rm list}a_{\rm assign}/2^{30}$; substituting
$a_{\rm in}$ would give an incorrect general model.}
\label{tab:b-scratch}
\footnotesize\input{tables/full-review/scratch}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

\subsection{Contemporaneous precision anchors}
\label{sec:b-precision-anchors}

T1 is a later campaign than D: it remeasures JHQ and competing baselines
near recall 0.97 in one session, with the E1 workspace pools enabled.
Each JHQ configuration has two repetitions bracketing the baselines;
each repetition uses five timing rounds. Per-configuration QPS combines
the two round medians geometrically. There are twelve paired configurations
per OpenAI dataset. The FP32/TF32 geometric means are 0.956/0.964;
both variants reuse a TF32-trained cache and change add/query arithmetic.

\par\medskip\noindent\begin{minipage}{\linewidth}
\centering\captionsetup{hypcap=false}
\captionof{table}[Same-session precision anchors]{T1 at recall 0.97 and batch 1,000.
Baseline columns report interpolation / strict measured threshold;
the FP32/TF32 column is interpolated. All methods here are contemporaneous;
these values are separate from D's cross-session baseline ratios.}
\label{tab:b-precision-anchors}
\footnotesize\setlength{\tabcolsep}{3pt}
\input{tables/full-review/precision-anchors}
\end{minipage}\par\medskip

Both estimators retain a numerical FP32 advantage in this finite grid.
The OpenAI-3072 RaBitQ bracket spans recall 0.9669--0.9796, exposing
substantial interpolation sensitivity. These anchor sweeps do not replace
E1's full grid, establish a universal margin, or constitute FP32 retraining.
The raw JSON and reconstruction are mapped in
\path{data/full-review/audit.json}.

\section{Artifact Access}
\label{sec:b-artifacts}
The report repository contains the parameter registry, derived tables,
figure sources, analysis scripts and 209-file v71 source/measurement
bundle under \path{evidence/ch5/v71/}. The offline command
\texttt{python3}\ \path{evidence/ch5/v71/check.py} verifies that bundle.
Selected T4/T5, T1 and A2 source/record inputs are also mirrored in
\path{evidence/review/}. Its checker verifies hashes and can rerun the
two corresponding offline audits using that local source root.
The broader raw records remain in the sibling \texttt{JHQ\_GPU} project
at the ledger paths. Complete vectors, trained states and all executables
are not packaged here. Offline recomputation requires the stated inputs;
GPU reproduction also requires the corresponding build and environment.

